The pictures are really paintings.

00:24:33.010 --> 00:24:36.910
They're filled with details of mountains and hills,

00:24:37.550 --> 00:24:41.630
ice skating and ice fishing, and people playing in the snow.

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:44.700
Some of the paintings even have signs you can read.

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:48.940
What I really liked about this book was that I felt like I was walking

00:24:48.940 --> 00:24:52.940
in a small town in Vermont, watching horse buggies go by.

00:24:53.450 --> 00:24:56.490
I promised you I'd tell you where my favorite winter place was.

00:24:56.630 --> 00:24:59.620
Well, it snuggled up on my bed, reading a good book.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:01.280
That's where I read this one.

00:25:02.460 --> 00:25:03.680
Hi, boys and girls.

00:25:03.880 --> 00:25:08.260
This is Ernie Perez telling you about an unusual book called Round Trip.

00:25:08.520 --> 00:25:09.900
Most trips are round trips.

00:25:10.120 --> 00:25:12.340
You go to a place and you come back home.

00:25:12.540 --> 00:25:15.360
But in this book, there's a little twist.

00:25:15.950 --> 00:25:20.550
The twist is you read the book all the way to the last page.

00:25:20.750 --> 00:25:25.180
Then you turn the whole book around and you read it the other way.

00:25:25.180 --> 00:25:29.980
On the trip we passed small farms, fields of

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.780
and you could even count the cars on a train.

00:25:33.370 --> 00:25:35.870
Very soon you get to a big city.

00:25:36.220 --> 00:25:39.880
My favorite picture is when they stop the car on the highway

00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:42.360
and they look at the fireworks in the sky.

00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:44.560
But when you turn the book around,

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:46.820
it looks like marshy inlets.

00:25:47.620 --> 00:25:50.520
You should read Round Trip because it's almost as good

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:51.980
as having two books.

00:25:52.380 --> 00:25:55.600
And it's one of the most unusual books I've ever read.

00:25:55.740 --> 00:25:56.880
You'll think so too.

00:25:56.880 --> 00:25:59.880
One thing never changed.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:05.450
People love to have fun. And here at Old Sturbridge Village, joining in is a snap.

00:26:06.700 --> 00:26:10.060
In some ways, maybe the olden days were the golden days.

00:26:10.390 --> 00:26:13.550
People live closer to the land, more in harmony with nature.

00:26:14.100 --> 00:26:18.350
And the world seem more their own, because they crafted so much of it by hand.

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:22.560
Looking back to see how far you've come shows how far you can go.

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:26.180
And there are always new frontiers, but you have to find them.

00:26:26.780 --> 00:26:28.480
You're the new pioneers.

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:29.980
LaVar, come join us.

00:26:30.660 --> 00:26:31.860
See you next time.

00:27:53.930 --> 00:27:59.980
books are Oxcart Man by Donald Hall, illustrated by Barbara Cooney, published by

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:06.360
Viking Penguin. Round Trip by Ann Jonas, published by Greenwillow Books. A Winter

00:28:06.360 --> 00:28:12.170
Place by Ruth Yaffe Radin, illustrated by Matty Lue O'Kelly, published by Little

00:28:12.170 --> 00:28:14.920
Brown. Wagon Wheels by Barbara Bren...

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:18.990
illustrated by Don Boulinets, published by Harper and Row.
